    "thm-shared-parameter-backpropagation-gradient-formula": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-straight-line-computational-program",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an input dimension $n\\ge0$, finitely many operation nodes $1,\\ldots,T$, and a designated output node $o$, either an input coordinate or an operation, whose value space is $\\mathbb R$. Give input coordinate $i$ a label $\\mathrm{in}_i$ distinct from all operation indices, and set $z_{\\mathrm{in}_i}=x_i$ and $E_{\\mathrm{in}_i}=\\mathbb R$ with its standard Euclidean inner product. Operation node $j$ has an ordered list of parent slots $p_{j,1},\\ldots,p_{j,r_j}$; each slot is either an input label $\\mathrm{in}_i$ or an operation index $\\ell<j$. Repeated parent slots are allowed and count separately. The graph is a finite straight-line program because every operation reads only earlier values and there are no data-dependent loops.\n\nFor each operation $j$, choose a dimension $d_j\\ge0$, set $E_j=\\mathbb R^{d_j}$ with its standard Euclidean inner product, take an open set $U_j\\subseteq E_{p_{j,1}}\\times\\cdots\\times E_{p_{j,r_j}}$ with the product inner product, and choose a primitive map $\\phi_j:U_j\\to E_j$. A smooth primitive is $C^1$: it is totally differentiable and its derivative matrix is continuous. For Hessian-vector products require the primitive to be $C^2$, meaning $C^1$ with a continuously differentiable derivative map. Derivatives are represented in these coordinates, and every transpose is the Euclidean transpose for the stated inner products; the gradient of a scalar output is the Euclidean gradient. The real-arithmetic primal recursion is $z_j=\\phi_j(z_{p_{j,1}},\\ldots,z_{p_{j,r_j}})$ whenever the parent tuple lies in $U_j$. Its admissible input domain is the set of $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ for which every operation in this recursion is defined. The graph's scalar output function $f$ is the value at $o$: it is $x_i$ when $o$ is input coordinate $i$, and $z_j$ when $o$ is operation node $j$.\n\nThe **primal tape** is the finite ordered record of input and node values, parent-slot indices, primitive identities, and (when applicable) selected branch labels. A deterministic replay starts from the identical checkpoint state and applies the identical primitive maps in the identical order, so it reproduces the same real-arithmetic values and local derivative inputs. Opaque solver calls are not primitive operations unless their complete deterministic map and derivative rule are explicitly supplied.\n\nA piecewise-$C^1$ primitive is specified by finitely many open branch regions and a $C^1$ formula on each region; its primal boundary value, if any, is specified separately. If the current parent tuple lies strictly inside exactly one declared branch, the program uses that branch and its derivative. At a branch boundary or ambiguous branch selection, forward or reverse AD returns NONDIFFERENTIABLE; it does not silently choose a subgradient or generalized derivative. If no primal value is specified at a boundary, that input is outside the program's domain. All correctness theorems below apply on a fixed strict branch. The same convention applies to piecewise-$C^2$ primitives for Hessian-vector products.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-backpropagation-through-shared-parameter-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p,q\\ge1$, let $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^p$ and $H\\subseteq\\mathbb R^q$ be open, let $T\\ge0$ be an integer, and fix $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. A map is continuously differentiable here when it is totally differentiable at every point of its open domain and its derivative matrix varies continuously there. A finite shared-parameter recurrence consists of an initial-state map $h_0:\\Theta\\to H$, transition maps $\\Phi_t:\\Theta\\times H\\to H$ for $t=1,\\ldots,T$, and a scalar loss $\\ell:\\Theta\\times H\\to\\mathbb R$, all continuously differentiable on open domains containing the evaluated inputs. Put $h_0=h_0(\\theta)$, $h_t=\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, and $J(\\theta)=\\ell(\\theta,h_T)$. A fixed collection of external inputs, such as the observations at successive time steps, may be built into the maps $\\Phi_t$. The same parameter vector $\\theta$ is supplied to every transition.\n\nFor each map, its registered local derivative is the total derivative with respect to the indicated variable while the other displayed variables are held fixed. Transposes are Euclidean adjoints for the stated coordinate inner products. Evaluate and store $h_0,h_1,\\ldots,h_T$. If a map is undefined, return DOMAIN_ERROR. For piecewise-$C^1$ maps, use only the derivative on a unique strict active branch; if any evaluated input lies on a branch boundary or has ambiguous branch selection, return NONDIFFERENTIABLE and no gradient.\n\nInitialize $\\lambda_T=(D_h\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T$ and $g=(D_\\theta\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T$. For $t=T,T-1,\\ldots,1$, set $A_t=D_\\theta\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, $B_t=D_h\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, then update\n\n$$g\\leftarrow g+A_t^T\\lambda_t,\\qquad \\lambda_{t-1}=B_t^T\\lambda_t.$$\n\nAfter the reverse sweep, set $g\\leftarrow g+(Dh_0(\\theta))^T\\lambda_0$ and return $(J(\\theta),g)$. For $T=0$, the reverse loop is empty and the same initialization and final update apply. All derivative calculations in this procedure are real-arithmetic operations on the specified maps; no floating-point accuracy guarantee is asserted.\n\nThe diagram shows the finite unrolling: arrows point from an input value to the operation that uses it. The single $\\theta$ node feeds each transition and the loss, so the reverse updates add all of those contributions into the same accumulator $g$; the $h_t$ cotangents flow from right to left. The diagram illustrates the algorithm, and no correctness proof relies on it.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.55cm]\n  \\node[draw,circle] (theta) {$\\theta$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=theta,xshift=0.4cm] (h0) {$h_0(\\theta)$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=h0,xshift=0.65cm] (phi1) {$\\Phi_1$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=phi1,xshift=0.65cm] (h1) {$h_1$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=h1,xshift=0.65cm] (phi2) {$\\Phi_2$};\n  \\node[right of=phi2,xshift=0.35cm] (dots) {$\\cdots$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=dots,xshift=0.35cm] (phit) {$\\Phi_T$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=phit,xshift=0.65cm] (ht) {$h_T$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=ht,xshift=0.65cm] (loss) {$\\ell(\\theta,h_T)$};\n  \\draw[->] (theta) -- (h0);\n  \\draw[->] (h0) -- (phi1);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=30] (phi1);\n  \\draw[->] (phi1) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (h1) -- (phi2);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=42] (phi2);\n  \\draw[->] (phi2) -- (dots);\n  \\draw[->] (dots) -- (phit);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=55] (phit);\n  \\draw[->] (phit) -- (ht);\n  \\draw[->] (ht) -- (loss);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=68] (loss);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-reverse-mode-computes-gradient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a finite $C^1$ program with one scalar output $f$, on a fixed strict branch and at every admissible $x$, the reverse-mode algorithm returns $Df(x)^T1$, the gradient of $f$. More precisely, at any operation node $i$, if its value is temporarily treated as an independent variable and all later operations are recomputed from it, then the accumulated cotangent at $i$ is the derivative of the scalar output with respect to that independent node value.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
