    "ex-checkpointed-reverse-sweep-recomputes-intermediate": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "alg-checkpointed-reverse-schedules",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The input is a finite sequence of deterministic $C^1$ transitions $F_j:U_j\\subseteq E_{j-1}\\to E_j$, $j=1,\\ldots,T$, between compatible finite-dimensional Euclidean spaces with open domains, a starting state $x_0\\in E_0$, and a terminal cotangent $\\bar x_T\\in E_T$. Every transition is evaluated on one fixed strict branch and has its exact local reverse rule. For $T\\ge1$, require the last transition's output space to be $E_T=\\mathbb R$; when that scalar output is the objective, take $\\bar x_T=1$. For $T=0$, the terminal value is $x_0$ and its matching cotangent $\\bar x_0$ is supplied. A transition tape stores the local values needed by its exact reverse rule. A deterministic replay applies the same transition maps, in the same order, to the identical checkpoint state and therefore reproduces the same values and local derivative inputs. The procedure returns the terminal value and cotangent at $x_0$; an invalid domain evaluation returns DOMAIN_ERROR, and a branch boundary returns NONDIFFERENTIABLE. A checkpoint schedule is permitted only when its saved states and all deterministic replays lie in these declared domains.\n\n**Uniform-block mode.** For $T=0$, return $x_0$ and the supplied cotangent without work. For $T\\ge1$, require an integer slot budget $m\\ge1$, set $q=\\lceil T/m\\rceil$, and during one forward pass save $x_{jq}$ for $j=0,\\ldots,\\lceil T/q\\rceil-1$ (all indices are below $T$). This uses at most $m$ saved states. Partition the steps into blocks $[a,b]$ with $a=jq$ and $b=\\min(a+q,T)$. Process the blocks from last to first. Within one block, for $\\ell=b,b-1,\\ldots,a+1$, restore $x_a$, replay $F_{a+1},\\ldots,F_{\\ell-1}$ without recording, evaluate $F_\\ell$ once while recording its local tape, apply its reverse rule to the current cotangent, and discard the one-step tape and replay workspace. Retain only the checkpoint states, the live state/cotangent, and the ordinary one-step reverse tape. Do not infer any optimality property of this schedule.\n\n**Binomial mode.** Define $C(d,0)=C(0,t)=1$, and $C(d,t)=C(d-1,t)+C(d,t-1)$ for integers $d,t\\ge1$. The top-level call takes nonnegative integer budgets $d,t$ satisfying $T\\le C(d,t)$. For $T=0$, return $(x_0,\\bar x_0)$. For $T\\ge1$, allocate one scalar output register $y$. The recursive procedure REVERSE$(a,N,d,t,x_a,\\bar x_{a+N})$ consumes its starting state as the live primal state and its terminal cotangent as the mutable cotangent buffer; it returns only the cotangent at $x_a$, retaining no terminal primal state. It requires integer indices $0\\le a\\le a+N\\le T$, nonnegative integer budgets $d,t$, and $N\\le C(d,t)$. If $N=0$, return the unchanged cotangent. If $N=1$, evaluate and record the unique transition, copy its scalar output to $y$ if $a+1=T$, apply its local reverse rule, discard the tape and primal state, and return the input cotangent. If $N\\ge2$, then $d,t\\ge1$. Put $L=\\min(N-1,C(d,t-1))$, $R=N-L$. Save $x_a$ in one checkpoint slot and replay the first $L$ transitions into the live state $x_{a+L}$ without recording. Call REVERSE$(a+L,R,d-1,t,x_{a+L},\\bar x_{a+N})$ on the right block, keeping the saved $x_a$ but no other primal state in the caller. Move the saved $x_a$ back into the live state, release its checkpoint slot, and tail-call REVERSE$(a,L,d,t-1,x_a,\\bar x_{a+L})$, using the cotangent returned by the right child. Return this left child's cotangent. No call frame retains a primal vector outside the explicit checkpoint slots. Invoke this procedure at $a=0,N=T$ and return $y$ with its returned initial cotangent. A recursive call with $N>1$ and $d=0$ or $t=0$ is a schedule failure; the capacity condition makes that branch unreachable.\n\nThe parameter $d$ bounds the number of simultaneously saved state snapshots. The parameter $t$ bounds, for each transition, the number of additional unrecorded forward evaluations beyond its one recorded evaluation. Both are finite integer inputs. This mode provides no numerical error estimate or optimality guarantee for arbitrary nonuniform primitive costs. For the storage count in the paired theorem, $R_s$ bounds scalar storage for one full primal state. The binomial recursion may reuse one mutable cotangent workspace: each call consumes its terminal cotangent and returns the cotangent at its start, reusing the same buffer through both children. Let $G$ bound the largest one-step tape and local reverse scratch, the constant number of live cotangent/work vectors, the scalar output register, and the scalar call-frame records; it excludes the $d$ saved primal states and one live primal state.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-checkpointed-reverse-schedules-preserve-adjoints-and-cost-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the finite-dimensional $C^1$ transition sequences on a fixed strict branch defined in [[alg-checkpointed-reverse-schedules]], provided every schedule replay remains in the declared domains, both schedules return the same terminal value and initial-state cotangent as an uncheckpointed reverse sweep. In uniform-block mode, if $T,m\\ge1$, $q=\\lceil T/m\\rceil$, and the block lengths are $r_1,\\ldots,r_s$, excluding the initial forward pass used to save checkpoints, the number of additional unrecorded forward evaluations is exactly $\\sum_{\\ell=1}^s r_\\ell(r_\\ell-1)/2$, at most $m q(q-1)/2$. At most $m$ checkpoint states are saved, with one live replay state and one local step tape. In binomial mode, $C(d,t)=\\binom{d+t}{d}$; any $T\\le C(d,t)$ steps can be reversed with at most $d$ saved snapshots, one recorded forward evaluation and one reverse evaluation per step, at most $t$ additional unrecorded forward evaluations per step, total work at most $\\widehat W+\\overline W+tW$, and storage $G+(d+1)R_s$. Here $W$ is the total cost of one unrecorded full forward pass, $\\widehat W$ the sum of recorded one-step forward costs, $\\overline W$ the sum of local reverse costs, $R_s$ bounds scalar storage for one primal state, and $G$ bounds the largest one-step tape and local reverse scratch, the constant number of live cotangent/work vectors, the scalar output register, and scalar recursion-frame records, excluding saved states and one live primal state.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-sum-product-and-composition-rules-for-banach-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U \\subseteq X$ be open in a real Banach space $X$, and let $Y$, $Z$, $W$ be\nreal Banach spaces. Then:\n\n1. **Sum rule.** If $f_1, f_2 : U \\to Y$ are Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x \\in U$\n   and $a, b \\in \\mathbb R$, then $af_1+bf_2$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$\n   with $D(af_1+bf_2)(x) = a\\,Df_1(x) + b\\,Df_2(x)$.\n2. **Bounded-bilinear product rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ and $g : U \\to Z$ are\n   Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, and $B : Y \\times Z \\to W$ is bounded\n   bilinear, then $U \\ni u \\mapsto B(f(u),g(u))$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at\n   $x$ and\n\n$$D\\bigl(B(f,g)\\bigr)(x)h = B\\bigl(Df(x)h,\\ g(x)\\bigr) + B\\bigl(f(x),\\ Dg(x)h\\bigr) \\qquad (h \\in X).$$\n\n3. **Chain rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, if\n   $W_0 \\subseteq Y$ is an open set with $f[U] \\subseteq W_0$, and if\n   $g : W_0 \\to Z$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $f(x)$, then\n   $g \\circ f$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$ and\n   $$D(g \\circ f)(x) = Dg\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)\\,Df(x).$$\n\nNo continuity of any derivative map is assumed; these are pointwise statements\nabout one $x$ at a time.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-total-differentiability-gives-a-local-linear-bound-and-continuity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is totally differentiable at $a\\in U$, then some $C,\\delta>0$ satisfy $\\|f(a+h)-f(a)\\|_2\\le C\\|h\\|_2$ whenever $\\|h\\|_2<\\delta$ and $a+h\\in U$. In particular $f$ is continuous at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "checkpoint-layout",
          "claim": "Here $q=\\lceil T/m\\rceil=2$, so the uniform-block schedule stores only $x_0=x$ and has one block $[0,2]$. In the reverse pass it first restores $x_0$ and replays $F_1$, obtaining $x_1=x^2$ before recording the local tape for $F_2$; it then restores $x_0$ again to process $F_1$. Both transitions are defined on all of $\\mathbb R$, so every replay stays in its declared domain.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-pullbacks",
          "claim": "Starting with output cotangent $\\bar x_2=1$, the local derivative of $F_2$ gives $\\bar x_1=\\cos(x^2)$ by [F5], and the local derivative of $F_1$ gives $\\bar x_0=2x\\cos(x^2)$ by [F4]. This includes $x=0$, where the derivative is $0$, and $x=1$, where it is $2\\cos(1)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
