    "ex-relu-kink-does-not-have-classical-ad-gradient": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-straight-line-computational-program",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an input dimension $n\\ge0$, finitely many operation nodes $1,\\ldots,T$, and a designated output node $o$, either an input coordinate or an operation, whose value space is $\\mathbb R$. Give input coordinate $i$ a label $\\mathrm{in}_i$ distinct from all operation indices, and set $z_{\\mathrm{in}_i}=x_i$ and $E_{\\mathrm{in}_i}=\\mathbb R$ with its standard Euclidean inner product. Operation node $j$ has an ordered list of parent slots $p_{j,1},\\ldots,p_{j,r_j}$; each slot is either an input label $\\mathrm{in}_i$ or an operation index $\\ell<j$. Repeated parent slots are allowed and count separately. The graph is a finite straight-line program because every operation reads only earlier values and there are no data-dependent loops.\n\nFor each operation $j$, choose a dimension $d_j\\ge0$, set $E_j=\\mathbb R^{d_j}$ with its standard Euclidean inner product, take an open set $U_j\\subseteq E_{p_{j,1}}\\times\\cdots\\times E_{p_{j,r_j}}$ with the product inner product, and choose a primitive map $\\phi_j:U_j\\to E_j$. A smooth primitive is $C^1$: it is totally differentiable and its derivative matrix is continuous. For Hessian-vector products require the primitive to be $C^2$, meaning $C^1$ with a continuously differentiable derivative map. Derivatives are represented in these coordinates, and every transpose is the Euclidean transpose for the stated inner products; the gradient of a scalar output is the Euclidean gradient. The real-arithmetic primal recursion is $z_j=\\phi_j(z_{p_{j,1}},\\ldots,z_{p_{j,r_j}})$ whenever the parent tuple lies in $U_j$. Its admissible input domain is the set of $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ for which every operation in this recursion is defined. The graph's scalar output function $f$ is the value at $o$: it is $x_i$ when $o$ is input coordinate $i$, and $z_j$ when $o$ is operation node $j$.\n\nThe **primal tape** is the finite ordered record of input and node values, parent-slot indices, primitive identities, and (when applicable) selected branch labels. A deterministic replay starts from the identical checkpoint state and applies the identical primitive maps in the identical order, so it reproduces the same real-arithmetic values and local derivative inputs. Opaque solver calls are not primitive operations unless their complete deterministic map and derivative rule are explicitly supplied.\n\nA piecewise-$C^1$ primitive is specified by finitely many open branch regions and a $C^1$ formula on each region; its primal boundary value, if any, is specified separately. If the current parent tuple lies strictly inside exactly one declared branch, the program uses that branch and its derivative. At a branch boundary or ambiguous branch selection, forward or reverse AD returns NONDIFFERENTIABLE; it does not silently choose a subgradient or generalized derivative. If no primal value is specified at a boundary, that input is outside the program's domain. All correctness theorems below apply on a fixed strict branch. The same convention applies to piecewise-$C^2$ primitives for Hessian-vector products.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
