        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-backpropagation-through-shared-parameter-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p,q\\ge1$, let $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^p$ and $H\\subseteq\\mathbb R^q$ be open, let $T\\ge0$ be an integer, and fix $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. A map is continuously differentiable here when it is totally differentiable at every point of its open domain and its derivative matrix varies continuously there. A finite shared-parameter recurrence consists of an initial-state map $h_0:\\Theta\\to H$, transition maps $\\Phi_t:\\Theta\\times H\\to H$ for $t=1,\\ldots,T$, and a scalar loss $\\ell:\\Theta\\times H\\to\\mathbb R$, all continuously differentiable on open domains containing the evaluated inputs. Put $h_0=h_0(\\theta)$, $h_t=\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, and $J(\\theta)=\\ell(\\theta,h_T)$. A fixed collection of external inputs, such as the observations at successive time steps, may be built into the maps $\\Phi_t$. The same parameter vector $\\theta$ is supplied to every transition.\n\nFor each map, its registered local derivative is the total derivative with respect to the indicated variable while the other displayed variables are held fixed. Transposes are Euclidean adjoints for the stated coordinate inner products. Evaluate and store $h_0,h_1,\\ldots,h_T$. If a map is undefined, return DOMAIN_ERROR. For piecewise-$C^1$ maps, use only the derivative on a unique strict active branch; if any evaluated input lies on a branch boundary or has ambiguous branch selection, return NONDIFFERENTIABLE and no gradient.\n\nInitialize $\\lambda_T=(D_h\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T$ and $g=(D_\\theta\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T$. For $t=T,T-1,\\ldots,1$, set $A_t=D_\\theta\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, $B_t=D_h\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, then update\n\n$$g\\leftarrow g+A_t^T\\lambda_t,\\qquad \\lambda_{t-1}=B_t^T\\lambda_t.$$\n\nAfter the reverse sweep, set $g\\leftarrow g+(Dh_0(\\theta))^T\\lambda_0$ and return $(J(\\theta),g)$. For $T=0$, the reverse loop is empty and the same initialization and final update apply. All derivative calculations in this procedure are real-arithmetic operations on the specified maps; no floating-point accuracy guarantee is asserted.\n\nThe diagram shows the finite unrolling: arrows point from an input value to the operation that uses it. The single $\\theta$ node feeds each transition and the loss, so the reverse updates add all of those contributions into the same accumulator $g$; the $h_t$ cotangents flow from right to left. The diagram illustrates the algorithm, and no correctness proof relies on it.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.55cm]\n  \\node[draw,circle] (theta) {$\\theta$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=theta,xshift=0.4cm] (h0) {$h_0(\\theta)$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=h0,xshift=0.65cm] (phi1) {$\\Phi_1$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=phi1,xshift=0.65cm] (h1) {$h_1$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=h1,xshift=0.65cm] (phi2) {$\\Phi_2$};\n  \\node[right of=phi2,xshift=0.35cm] (dots) {$\\cdots$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=dots,xshift=0.35cm] (phit) {$\\Phi_T$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=phit,xshift=0.65cm] (ht) {$h_T$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=ht,xshift=0.65cm] (loss) {$\\ell(\\theta,h_T)$};\n  \\draw[->] (theta) -- (h0);\n  \\draw[->] (h0) -- (phi1);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=30] (phi1);\n  \\draw[->] (phi1) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (h1) -- (phi2);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=42] (phi2);\n  \\draw[->] (phi2) -- (dots);\n  \\draw[->] (dots) -- (phit);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=55] (phit);\n  \\draw[->] (phit) -- (ht);\n  \\draw[->] (ht) -- (loss);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=68] (loss);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "alg-forward-mode-automatic-differentiation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a graph from [[def-finite-straight-line-computational-program]], an admissible point $x$, and a direction $v\\in\\mathbb R^n$. Require every primitive to be $C^1$ on its declared open domain or supplied as a piecewise-$C^1$ primitive with the branch rules of the graph definition. Evaluate all primal node values in topological order and store the tape. Set each input tangent to $\\dot z_{\\mathrm{in}_i}=\\dot x_i=v_i$, using the distinct input labels of the graph. For each operation $j=1,\\ldots,T$, compute\n$$\\dot z_j=D\\phi_j(z_{p_{j,1}},\\ldots,z_{p_{j,r_j}})(\\dot z_{p_{j,1}},\\ldots,\\dot z_{p_{j,r_j}}).$$\nReturn the primal scalar output $f(x)$ and its tangent: return $(z_o,\\dot z_o)$ if $o$ is an operation node, and $(x_i,v_i)$ if $o$ is input coordinate $i$. If the input is outside the admissible domain, return DOMAIN_ERROR; if a piecewise primitive is at a branch boundary or has no unique strict active branch, return NONDIFFERENTIABLE and no tangent. All derivative evaluations in this definition are exact real-arithmetic operations on the specified primitive maps.",
