    "ex-shared-weight-neural-backpropagation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-feedforward-neural-network",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $d,r\\ge1$. A finite feedforward computation graph from $\\mathbb R^d$ to $\\mathbb R^r$ consists of a finite directed graph $(V,E)$ with no directed cycle; pairwise disjoint input, hidden, and output vertex sets $I,H,O$ whose union is $V$; exactly $d$ input vertices and $r$ output vertices; and a specified ordering of $V$ in which the tail of every directed edge precedes its head. The input vertices are labelled bijectively by the coordinates $1,\\ldots,d$, and input vertex $i$ has value $x_i$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Each non-input vertex $v$ has a real bias $b_v$, a real weight $w_{uv}$ for every edge $u\\to v$, and a real activation function $\\rho_v$. In the specified order, its value is\n\n$$z_v=\\rho_v\\!\\left(b_v+\\sum_{u:\\,u\\to v}w_{uv}z_u\\right).$$\n\nThe network output is the vector of values at $O$. A layered multilayer perceptron is the specialization with layers $V_0,\\ldots,V_L$, where $V_0=I$, $V_L=O$, and every edge goes from $V_{\\ell-1}$ to $V_\\ell$ for some $\\ell$; the hidden vertices use a shared activation and the output vertices use the identity activation. A fully connected layered perceptron includes every edge between consecutive layers. The fixed ordering makes the recursive evaluation unambiguous and is part of the graph data.\n\nThe diagram shows the edge direction and evaluation convention for a fully connected scalar-output perceptron with one hidden layer. Each circle is one scalar-valued node; the hidden nodes apply $\\sigma$ and the output node is linear. The diagram illustrates the definition and is not used in any proof.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.5cm]\n  \\node[draw,circle] (x1) {$x_1$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,below of=x1] (x2) {$x_2$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,right of=x1,yshift=-0.35cm] (h1) {$\\sigma$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,below of=h1] (h2) {$\\sigma$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,right of=h1,yshift=-0.35cm] (y) {$y$};\n  \\draw[->] (x1) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (x1) -- (h2);\n  \\draw[->] (x2) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (x2) -- (h2);\n  \\draw[->] (h1) -- (y);\n  \\draw[->] (h2) -- (y);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-backpropagation-through-shared-parameter-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p,q\\ge1$, let $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^p$ and $H\\subseteq\\mathbb R^q$ be open, let $T\\ge0$ be an integer, and fix $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. A map is continuously differentiable here when it is totally differentiable at every point of its open domain and its derivative matrix varies continuously there. A finite shared-parameter recurrence consists of an initial-state map $h_0:\\Theta\\to H$, transition maps $\\Phi_t:\\Theta\\times H\\to H$ for $t=1,\\ldots,T$, and a scalar loss $\\ell:\\Theta\\times H\\to\\mathbb R$, all continuously differentiable on open domains containing the evaluated inputs. Put $h_0=h_0(\\theta)$, $h_t=\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, and $J(\\theta)=\\ell(\\theta,h_T)$. A fixed collection of external inputs, such as the observations at successive time steps, may be built into the maps $\\Phi_t$. The same parameter vector $\\theta$ is supplied to every transition.\n\nFor each map, its registered local derivative is the total derivative with respect to the indicated variable while the other displayed variables are held fixed. Transposes are Euclidean adjoints for the stated coordinate inner products. Evaluate and store $h_0,h_1,\\ldots,h_T$. If a map is undefined, return DOMAIN_ERROR. For piecewise-$C^1$ maps, use only the derivative on a unique strict active branch; if any evaluated input lies on a branch boundary or has ambiguous branch selection, return NONDIFFERENTIABLE and no gradient.\n\nInitialize $\\lambda_T=(D_h\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T$ and $g=(D_\\theta\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T$. For $t=T,T-1,\\ldots,1$, set $A_t=D_\\theta\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, $B_t=D_h\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, then update\n\n$$g\\leftarrow g+A_t^T\\lambda_t,\\qquad \\lambda_{t-1}=B_t^T\\lambda_t.$$\n\nAfter the reverse sweep, set $g\\leftarrow g+(Dh_0(\\theta))^T\\lambda_0$ and return $(J(\\theta),g)$. For $T=0$, the reverse loop is empty and the same initialization and final update apply. All derivative calculations in this procedure are real-arithmetic operations on the specified maps; no floating-point accuracy guarantee is asserted.\n\nThe diagram shows the finite unrolling: arrows point from an input value to the operation that uses it. The single $\\theta$ node feeds each transition and the loss, so the reverse updates add all of those contributions into the same accumulator $g$; the $h_t$ cotangents flow from right to left. The diagram illustrates the algorithm, and no correctness proof relies on it.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.55cm]\n  \\node[draw,circle] (theta) {$\\theta$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=theta,xshift=0.4cm] (h0) {$h_0(\\theta)$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=h0,xshift=0.65cm] (phi1) {$\\Phi_1$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=phi1,xshift=0.65cm] (h1) {$h_1$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=h1,xshift=0.65cm] (phi2) {$\\Phi_2$};\n  \\node[right of=phi2,xshift=0.35cm] (dots) {$\\cdots$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=dots,xshift=0.35cm] (phit) {$\\Phi_T$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=phit,xshift=0.65cm] (ht) {$h_T$};\n  \\node[draw,rectangle,right of=ht,xshift=0.65cm] (loss) {$\\ell(\\theta,h_T)$};\n  \\draw[->] (theta) -- (h0);\n  \\draw[->] (h0) -- (phi1);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=30] (phi1);\n  \\draw[->] (phi1) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (h1) -- (phi2);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=42] (phi2);\n  \\draw[->] (phi2) -- (dots);\n  \\draw[->] (dots) -- (phit);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=55] (phit);\n  \\draw[->] (phit) -- (ht);\n  \\draw[->] (ht) -- (loss);\n  \\draw[->] (theta) to[bend left=68] (loss);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-shared-parameter-backpropagation-gradient-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p,q\\ge1$, let $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^p$ and $H\\subseteq\\mathbb R^q$ be open, and let $T$ be a nonnegative integer. Suppose $h_0:\\Theta\\to H$, $\\Phi_t:\\Theta\\times H\\to H$ for $1\\le t\\le T$, and $\\ell:\\Theta\\times H\\to\\mathbb R$ are continuously differentiable on open domains containing the trajectory at $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. A continuously differentiable map is totally differentiable at each point and has a derivative matrix continuous on its domain. Define $h_0=h_0(\\theta)$, $h_t=\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, and $J(\\theta)=\\ell(\\theta,h_T)$. Write $A_t=D_\\theta\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$ and $B_t=D_h\\Phi_t(\\theta,h_{t-1})$, with each partial derivative holding the other argument fixed. Set $\\lambda_T=(D_h\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T$ and, in descending order, $\\lambda_{t-1}=B_t^T\\lambda_t$. Then the backpropagation algorithm returns $J(\\theta)$ and\n\n$$\\nabla_\\theta J(\\theta)=(D_\\theta\\ell(\\theta,h_T))^T+\\sum_{t=1}^{T}A_t^T\\lambda_t+(Dh_0(\\theta))^T\\lambda_0,$$\n\nwhere the sum is empty when $T=0$. The statement also applies when every primitive is evaluated on one fixed strict $C^1$ branch; it makes no assertion at a branch boundary.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-sum-product-and-composition-rules-for-banach-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U \\subseteq X$ be open in a real Banach space $X$, and let $Y$, $Z$, $W$ be\nreal Banach spaces. Then:\n\n1. **Sum rule.** If $f_1, f_2 : U \\to Y$ are Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x \\in U$\n   and $a, b \\in \\mathbb R$, then $af_1+bf_2$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$\n   with $D(af_1+bf_2)(x) = a\\,Df_1(x) + b\\,Df_2(x)$.\n2. **Bounded-bilinear product rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ and $g : U \\to Z$ are\n   Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, and $B : Y \\times Z \\to W$ is bounded\n   bilinear, then $U \\ni u \\mapsto B(f(u),g(u))$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at\n   $x$ and\n\n$$D\\bigl(B(f,g)\\bigr)(x)h = B\\bigl(Df(x)h,\\ g(x)\\bigr) + B\\bigl(f(x),\\ Dg(x)h\\bigr) \\qquad (h \\in X).$$\n\n3. **Chain rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, if\n   $W_0 \\subseteq Y$ is an open set with $f[U] \\subseteq W_0$, and if\n   $g : W_0 \\to Z$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $f(x)$, then\n   $g \\circ f$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$ and\n   $$D(g \\circ f)(x) = Dg\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)\\,Df(x).$$\n\nNo continuity of any derivative map is assumed; these are pointwise statements\nabout one $x$ at a time.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
